#use(std/include)
#use("ext/admonition.rkt")

#section{Extending Stargazer}::

A YAMD document is a Racket program. #tt{##(...)} on its own line is a host
statement; inline it is an expression whose value becomes content. And
#tt{## } opens a line comment, #tt{##;{...}} a block one — one of each hides
right here in the source, leaving no trace in the output:

# a line comment: dropped by the reader
#;{a block comment; braces must balance,
so it may span lines}

#(define release "2.3")

This handbook tracks Stargazer #(release).

Host code can generate content — #tt{##para{...}} re-enters markup from
inside the loop, and #tt{##(m)} inside that body sees the loop variable:

#(define messiers (quote ("M31" "M42" "M57")))
#(for/list ([m messiers])
	#para{Target #(m) is up tonight.})

The same trick builds list items (note: in host code, #tt{list} is YAMD's
#tt{##list} function, so plain data wants #tt{quote}):

#list(style: "a")
	#(for/list ([m messiers]) #item{#(m)})

This chapter also loads a #emph{project-local} plugin with a
document-relative #tt{##use} path — see #tt{ext/admonition.rkt}:

#admonition(kind: "warning"){A plugin runs with full host power. Only
#tt{##use} code you trust.}

Press #keys{Ctrl+Shift+P} to open the command palette; #keys{F5} re-plans.
The #tt{##keys} body is parsed by the plugin's custom body reader at read
time, not by the markup parser.

#section{Scoped context}::

#fork-context::

#(context-set! 'admonition-kind "tip")

Inside this #tt{##fork-context} block the context key set above makes every
bare #tt{##admonition} default to a tip; the setting dies with the block.

#admonition{Like this one.}

#admonition{And this one — still a tip, no #tt{kind:} argument.}

#include("common/colophon.yamd")
